{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":["program:reasoning-envelope-wf"],"disanalogies":["Standard linear transformations act on a static vector space, whereas elementary cellular automata have spatially structured, local neighborhoods mapped to states.","Rule 90 is temporally memoryless (depends only on t), while rule 54 depends on the state at both t and t-1, requiring an augmented state space to map linearly.","The wrap-around boundary in cellular automata imposes the specific quotient ring GF(2)[x]/(x^W - 1), which introduces repeated factors not present in standard unbounded vector spaces."],"overturn_conditions":["The transition matrix of rule 90 over GF(2) fails to diagonalize into blocks corresponding to cyclotomic polynomials modulo x^W - 1.","A width W is found where rule 90 reaches a cycle of length > 2 despite 2^ord_W(2) - 1 being mathematically small (e.g., yielding a 7-cycle instead of a 1-cycle for W=7).","The period of rule 54 for a given width W is found to be independent of the multiplicative order of 3 modulo the square-free part of W."],"shared_structure":["Both rule 90 and linear transformations over GF(2) operate by bitwise XOR without carrying.","The evolution of rule 90 corresponds precisely to multiplying the state vector by the tridiagonal matrix (I + S^2) over GF(2).","In both, the long-term behavior (periods and invariant subspaces) is completely determined by the minimal polynomial of the transition matrix, which factors via cyclotomic polynomials depending on the ring size (width W)."],"source_memory_refs":["linear-algebra-finite-fields"],"transfer_claims":["The invariant subspace decomposition predicts that rule 90 on a prime width W will have no non-zero transients; all states are purely periodic.","The algebraic mechanism implies that if 2 is a primitive root modulo W, the maximum cycle length for rule 90 will be exactly 2^(W-1) - 1, which vastly exceeds 4*W.","Rule 54, viewed as an additive rule with period-3 memory, will have its state space decomposition governed by the multiplicative order of 3 modulo W, producing cycle lengths dividing 3^ord - 1."]},"claim":"Under rules 90, 110, and 184 with wrap-around widths 5 through 11, only rule 90 has pairs (W = 5, 6, 7, 8, 11) where every state enters a cycle of length at most 2 within 4*W steps. Rule 184 exhibits much longer transients, but its right-shift dynamics ensure eventual synchronization, whereas rule 110 contains propagating gliders whose phase shift prevents short cycles. Rule 90's short-cycle behavior is driven by additive linearity over GF(2): the width-W state space decomposes into invariant subspaces determined by the order of 2 modulo W, causing the global period to divide 2^ord - 1, which is small for the specified W. Rule 54 is also additive over GF(2) but with period-3 temporal memory, so its period divides 3^ord - 1 instead.","counterconditions":["If a width W (between 5 and 11) is found for rule 90 where a state takes a transient longer than 4*W or enters a cycle longer than 2, the linearity claim fails.","If rule 184 or 110 is shown to enter cycles of length 1 or 2 for all initial states in any W between 5 and 11, the separating property is invalid.","If the cycle length of rule 90 does not rigorously match the algebraic prediction 2^ord_W(2) - 1 for a tested W, the GF(2) mechanism is falsified.","If simulating rule 54 in Python yields maximum cycle lengths diverging from the predicted period 3^ord_W(3) - 1, the generalization to additive memory rules is incorrect."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Rule 90's dynamics can be represented exactly as a linear transformation over GF(2) using the polynomial multiplier (1 + x^2). Under wrap-around (mod x^W - 1), the period of the global transition matrix is governed by the multiplicative order of 2 modulo the square-free part of W. For W = 7, the order of 2 is 3, so the global period divides 2^3 - 1 = 7. Because 7 is less than 2^4 = 16, all non-zero states live in cycles of length 7 (or 1 for the zero vector), and all transients are zero. Thus, the maximum cycle length is well below 2, achieving the bound within 4*W = 28 steps. This purely algebraic property cleanly separates rule 90 from rule 184 (which has additive constants breaking linearity) and rule 110 (which is strongly nonlinear). To verify, a sandboxed Python simulation should map the exact transition graph for each (rule, W) and file the full state-to-cycle-length table as proof of exhaustiveness.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-ec8ff8408ba123723cc304b0d62abd5188ddbdd9"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}